---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../components/Byline.astro";
import LegalNote from "../components/LegalNote.astro";
import { LINKS, CANON_PATHS, linkClass, ext } from "../lib/links";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";

const path = "/nics-is-not-sales";
const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "NICS is weather", path },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="They made the NICS case in 2024. Then they stopped the sentence. | FFL Intel"
  description="Gearfire put it on Outdoor Wire in June 2024: a background check is not a new-gun unit. A POS panel is cleaner. It is still not the ask."
  path={path}
  ogImagePath={CANON_PATHS.range}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-400 mb-3">
      Checks
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white text-balance mb-4">
      They made the NICS case in 2024. Then they stopped the sentence.
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-300 mb-6">
      Gearfire put the rebuttal on the wire. I am not going to pretend I
      discovered it. I am going to finish the clause they will not write.
    </p>
    <Byline dateLabel="August 13, 2026" read="8 min" />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800">
      <img
        src={withBase(CANON_PATHS.range)}
        alt="Betsy at the range"
        width="1280"
        height="720"
        class="w-full object-cover max-h-80"
      />
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif">
      <p>
        Betsy — night desk. For years the trade treated NICS like a cash
        register with extra steps. A monthly FBI count went up, and a
        production meeting behaved as if units had already left the dock. A
        monthly count went down, and someone called it a demand crisis. The
        check is weather. Weather is not a ledger.
      </p>
      <p>
        Gearfire said so, in public, on Outdoor Wire, June 6, 2024. They were
        at the NSSF Marketing & Leadership Summit with a Q1 report. Kaleb
        Seymour, VP of data analytics: a gap between the volume of background
        checks and the number of firearms sold. “This discrepancy could be
        attributed to various factors, including multiple purchases per check
        or denials, which the NICS reports do not address.” The same file
        listed the rest of the mess: permit applications, used firearm sales,
        transfers. That report said 2,500 contributing retailers. The Index
        About line later says more than two thousand. I am not litigating the
        panel. I am recording that they made the case before they had a
        monthly to sell.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every Index release since has restated it. Permits. Used guns.
        Transfers. Denials. Several guns on one check. A background check is
        not a new-gun unit. They are right.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then they built a same-store register series and asked the trade to
        retire NICS as the production compass. Fair, as far as the register
        goes. A POS panel of more than two thousand stores — their About line
        on the April 13, 2026 file — is a better sales series than FBI
        weather. It is still a panel. It is still last month. It still cannot
        see a search that returned nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the unfinished sentence. Checks are not sales. Units are not
        asks. You can have a quiet NICS month and a busy used-gun counter.
        You can have a down Index — March, minus 11.9 percent, Outdoor Wire,
        April 13 — and a dealer site at 9:40 p.m. full of people typing a
        model that is not on the shelf. Guns.com, on March 12, claimed local
        dealer sales on their platform up 46 percent in February and said
        pre-owned was about 31 percent of the mix. The Index is new firearms.
        Both can be true. Stapling them is how a forecast goes sideways.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the honest stack is three files, not two. Checks. Units. Asks. I
        will not run a NICS. I will not sit in their Sisense. I will not call
        either series “demand” without saying which kind. The rest of that
        argument, next to the product, is on GunSearchEngine:{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.gseNics} class={linkClass} {...ext}
          >NICS vs firearm sales</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>That’s the file.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-10"><LegalNote /></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
